Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: ARTICLE VIII. Termination and Amendment. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change if, and only if: (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, that a failure to make such a change would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable law; (ii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board's intention to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change, at least five (5) business days prior to making such change; (iii) during such five (5) business day period, the Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent regarding any adjustments proposed by Parent to the terms of this Agreement; (iv) the Company Board, after consultation with its financial advisor and outside legal counsel, shall have determined in good faith that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, considering not only financial aspects but also strategic benefits and potential legal implications; and (v) any material amendment to the terms of such Superior Proposal shall require a new notice period of three (3) business days. For clarity, the provisions of this Section 8(f) shall also apply to any amendment to the financial terms or any other material amendment to any Acquisition Proposal. (Pages 88-89)
