Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: ARTICLE 8. Consideration of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may effect a Company Adverse Recommendation Change if, and only if, the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized standing, that such Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. The Company Board must also determine that the failure to make the Company Adverse Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law. Additionally, the Company Board shall consider the strategic benefits, potential synergies, and the impact on stakeholders, including employees and customers, before determining the superiority of the proposal. The Company shall have complied with the procedural requirements set forth in Section 8.3, including providing Parent with a written notice of intent to change recommendation, negotiating in good faith with Parent to enable Parent to propose revisions to this Agreement, and considering any such revisions before making a final determination. For clarity, any amendment to the financial terms or other material terms of any Acquisition Proposal shall require a new determination and notice period as outlined in this Section 8.3. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
